    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im having some trouble with these walnut dovetails i'm cutting for a cabinet set. the pins are splintering a bit at the very tips when i try to pare them down to size. i've checked my chisels and they feel sharp, but i keep getting these tiny little crushed fibers instead of a clean cut. any idea what i might be doing wrong with my grain management or tool angle?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like you're experiencing the infamous \"pin splintering\" issue. One possible cause is that you're paring the pins too aggressively, which can cause the fibers to crush and splinter. Check your paring angles and speeds to ensure you're using the light touch necessary to remove only a tiny amount of wood, and also verify you're paring in the same direction as the grain to avoid splitting finely aligned fibers.",
