    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'm worried the wood might be too brittle for the fine detail work i'm doing. do you think i should try pre-wetting the end grain or maybe using a different type of chisel? i'm just trying to figure out if there's anything else i can do technique-wise to keep those tips from just turning into sawdust and flakes.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Trying to work with fine, brittle woods like walnut can be frustrating. Pre-wetting the end grain is a common technique that may help, but it's best done in small amounts to avoid introducing excess moisture and causing the wood to soften or become too planed. You may also consider switching to a finer-tooth chisel with a more pointed tip, which can help displace fibers and provide a cleaner cut. This can be a good compromise between cut-through and penetration.",
